<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vanga</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vanga was an ancient kingdom and region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> delta in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, roughly central and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>. It is named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> and other early texts, and the name Bengal is derived from it. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> Vanga was ruled by the Chandra, Varman and Sena dynasties from capitals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramapura">Vikramapura</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The kingdom of Vanga emerged in the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> delta during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-black-polished-ware">Northern Black Polished Ware</a> period. Its lands took in the many islands of the delta and the Sundarbans mangrove forest, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a> they reached the present Khulna, Barisal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a> divisions and areas east of the Old Brahmaputra. Its neighbours included Pundravardhana to the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>, Anga and Suhma to the west. Ancient texts name subdivisions such as Upa Vanga, corresponding to Jessore and the forests of the Sundarbans, and Anuttara Vanga around Barisal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ancient Indian records describe Vanga as a hub of sailors, and its fleet controlled many of the delta's islands. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthashastra">Arthashastra</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanakya">Kautilya</a> records it as an administrative unit, and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalidasa">Kalidasa</a> describes it as a naval power. Indian and Greco-Roman writers mention the region's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>, and it was probably the centre of the Gangaridai of the Greco-Roman authors. According to the story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-vijaya">Prince Vijaya</a>, the son of King Sinhabahu was expelled with 700 followers for violent deeds and sailed across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> in the 5th century BCE to found the Kingdom of Tambapanni in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>.</p><p>The exact capital is not known. Chandraketugarh, with continuous remains from the Pre-Mauryan to the Pala-Sena period, is considered a major city, and Jain texts name Tamralipta as the capital. Most of its rulers remain unknown, and after the 2nd century BCE the territory passed under successive powers, among them the Mauryans, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a>, the Palas, the Chandras and the Senas. After the Guptas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> was divided into the kingdoms of Gauda and Vanga, with Kotalipara as a centre of the Vanga kings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> the founders of Vanga, Anga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a>, Pundra and Suhma are sons of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, and the Vangas fight with elephants on the side of the Kauravas. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> names Vanga as an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a>. The name lived on as Vangala, Vangaladesha in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> inscription and Bangalah after the Muslim conquest, precursors of Banga and Bangla. Chandraketugarh, Tamralipta and Wari-Bateshwar are its main archaeological sites.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vanga kingdom” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Trailokyachandra extended their rule over <b>Vanga</b>, and his son Shrichandra moved the capital to Vikramapura near Dhaka, ruling a kingdom independent of the Pala Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/chandra-dynasty">Chandra dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Jatavarman, who married a princess of the Kalachuris of Tripuri, established their rule in <b>Vanga</b> as the Pala Empire weakened in the late 11th century, after the fall of the Chandra dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/varman-dynasty-of-bengal">Varman dynasty of Bengal</a></li></ul></section>
